  "mainEntity": {
    "@type": "Question",
    "name": "Is My Sagging Ceiling Drywall a Hazard After a Storm?",
    "text": "Is My Sagging Ceiling Drywall a Hazard After a Storm?",
    "answerCount": 1,
    "acceptedAnswer": {
      "@type": "Answer",
      "text": "Discovering your ceiling drywall is sagging after a storm can be pretty alarming, and yes, it absolutely can be a hazard! When ceiling drywall gets saturated with water, it absorbs that moisture, becomes much heavier, and loses its structural integrity. This can lead to a very real risk of collapse, which is obviously dangerous for anyone underneath. So, if you notice your ceiling drywall is sagging, your very first step should be to assess the structural safety of the area and consider it a potential immediate hazard. Beyond the immediate danger, sagging can also indicate more extensive damage, including potential issues with the framing or insulation above. It’s not just about aesthetics; it’s about the safety of your home. This kind of damage is definitely not a DIY project. If you've got sagging ceiling drywall, it's incredibly important to call in a qualified professional immediately. Experts like HH Remodeling LLC can properly inspect the damage, determine the underlying cause, and ensure the necessary repairs are done safely and correctly, protecting your family and your home.",
      "author": {
        "@type": "Organization",
        "name": "HH Remodeling LLC"
      }
    }
  },
